  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe will apply a two-phase optimization strategy using adapted versions of the previous program's techniques focused on a single sequence length. First, we generate an initial sequence of length 512 (chosen because it's large enough to achieve sub-2 values but not too large to be computationally infeasible). Then, we use an iterative process combining:\n\n- Linear Programming (LP) with adaptive tightening to enforce constraints and find binding inequalities\n- Log-sum-exp (LSE) gradient descent with momentum to guide updates toward minimizing the objective\n- Simulated annealing with gradually cooling temperatures to escape local minima\n- L-BFGS-B with the LSE surrogate objective for efficient global optimization\n\nThe algorithm is designed to work within a multiple restart framework (3 restarts) to explore different areas of the search space comprehensively. We prioritize diversity in the initial population and aggressive exploration during the annealing phase.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe consider sequences of fixed length n=512. The objective is J(a) = 2*n*M / s^2, where:\n- M = max(full linear convolution of a with itself)\n- s = sum of sequence elements (with implicit normalization to [0.01, None] in validate_after_convolve)\n\nFor optimization, we use the following surrogate:\nLSE(tau, a) = max_j convolve_j(a, a) + (1/tau) * log(sum exp(tau * (convole_j(a, a) - max_conv)))\n\nThe surrogate shares the same optimization direction as J but is computationally cheaper. Gradients are approximate but capture the sensitivity of LSE to the coefficients.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Generate parent sequence if available, else use no external sequence\n- Initialize with diverse sequences (length 512) using generate_initializations function\n- Run phases sequentially across restarts:\n  1. Initial diversification by generating multiple candidates of length 512\n  2. LP iterations with constraint tightening to enforce binding inequalities\n  3. LSE gradient descent with adaptive learning rates\n  4. Simulated annealing with decreasing temperature\n  5. L-BFGS-B polishing with surrogate objective\n- Maintain a running time estimate and strictly budget the phase execution\n- Score tracking ensures minimal failures and latest improvements are stored\n\n4. Search choices and hyperparameters\n- Length fixed at 512\n- Hyperparameter sweep:\n  - tau_range: [20.0, 50.0, 100.0] with adaptive momentum\n  - learning rate base: 0.5/(n*tau)\n  - temperature_range: [0.12, 0.065, 0.02] for annealing\n  - Initial population size: 9 diverse sequences\n  - Tolerance for convergence: 1e-15 to ensure precision\n  - Iteration budget: 500 for initial runs to 10^(exp - 3) for L-BFGS phases\n\n5. Computation budget\n- Total time: 1500 seconds\n- Run initial evaluations (evaluate_sequence) for each candidate\n- FFT operations for convolution dominate cost; conserve by using surrogate\n- Periodic fallback checks to validate and repair potential problem sequences\n- Adapt phases if time runs low: prioritize constraint tightening then gradients\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final candidate valid if all elements >=0, sum >=0.01\n- J <= 1.5027 required; else fall back to parent sequence\n- Last resort fallback: uniform sequence of length 512\n- Periodic calls to evaluate_sequence to ensure updates match\n- Extensive progress printing to track improvements and phase timings\n- Final post-optimization minimal random perturbations to escape potential plateaus",
